{"name":"Mesa Peach","hex":"#c19180","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mesa","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flat area of land higher than other land, with one or more clifflike edges.","a structure with components rising above the insulating substrate that surrounds it"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mesa"},{"term":"peach","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any tree of species Prunus persica, native to China and now widely cultivated throughout temperate regions, having pink flowers and edible fruit.","Soft juicy stone fruit of the peach tree, having yellow flesh, downy, red-tinted yellow skin, and a deeply sculptured pit or stone containing a single seed."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to the color peach.","Particularly pleasing or agreeable."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/peach"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mesa","blocks":[{"text":"A mesa is an isolated, flat-topped elevation, ridge, or hill, bounded from all sides by steep escarpments and standing distinctly above a surrounding plain. Mesas consist of flat-lying soft sedimentary rocks, such as shales, capped by a resistant layer of harder rock, like sandstone or limestone, forming a caprock that protects the flat summit. The caprock may also include dissected lava flows or eroded duricrust."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Over_Monument_Valley%2C_Navajo_Nation.jpg/330px-Over_Monument_Valley%2C_Navajo_Nation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mesa","wikidata":"Q623319"},{"title":"Peach","blocks":[{"text":"The peach is a deciduous tree that bears edible juicy fruits with various characteristics. Most are simply called peaches, while the glossy-skinned, non-fuzzy varieties are called nectarines. Though from the same species, they are regarded commercially as different fruits."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Illustration_Prunus_persica_clean_no_descr.jpg/330px-Illustration_Prunus_persica_clean_no_descr.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peach","wikidata":"Q13189"},{"title":"Peach (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Peach is a color that is named for the pale color of the interior flesh of the peach fruit. Like the color apricot, the color peach is paler than most actual peach fruits and seems to have been formulated primarily to create a pastel palette of colors for interior design."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peach_(color)","wikidata":"Q5148721"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/HarQatum.jpg/500px-HarQatum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/HarQatum.jpg/1280px-HarQatum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/HarQatum.jpg/500px-HarQatum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Har Qatum, a mesa located on the southern edge of Makhtesh Ramon, Israel","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHarQatum.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Ingleborough_from_Little_Ingleborough_-_geograph.org.uk_-_548604.jpg/500px-Ingleborough_from_Little_Ingleborough_-_geograph.org.uk_-_548604.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Ingleborough_from_Little_Ingleborough_-_geograph.org.uk_-_548604.jpg/500px-Ingleborough_from_Little_Ingleborough_-_geograph.org.uk_-_548604.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ingleborough in North Yorkshire, England","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIngleboro